## Recommended: Adopt `System.IO.Abstractions`

This is the established NuGet package for making filesystem statics testable — no need to write custom wrappers.

### 1. Install the package

```bash
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions
```

### 2. Register in DI

```csharp
// In Program.cs / Startup.cs
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IFileSystem, FileSystem>();
```

### 3. Inject `IFileSystem` into your classes

```csharp
public class MyService(IFileSystem fileSystem)
{
    public string Load(string path)
        => fileSystem.File.ReadAllText(path);

    public void Save(string path, string content)
        => fileSystem.File.WriteAllText(path, content);

    public bool EnsureDirectory(string path)
        => fileSystem.Directory.Exists(path);
}
```

### 4. Test with `MockFileSystem`

```bash
dotnet add <YourTestProject> package System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers
```

```csharp
var mockFs = new MockFileSystem(new Dictionary<string, MockFileData>
{
    { "/data/config.json", new MockFileData("{\"key\": \"value\"}") }
});

var service = new MyService(mockFs);

service.Save("/data/output.txt", "hello");
Assert.Equal("hello", mockFs.File.ReadAllText("/data/output.txt"));
Assert.True(service.EnsureDirectory("/data"));
```

---

**Why this over a custom wrapper?** `System.IO.Abstractions` is battle-tested, covers the full `System.IO` surface, and ships a `MockFileSystem` that handles paths, directories, and file content in-memory — so you don't have to maintain any of that yourself.

Once you've added the package and DI registration, you can use the **migrate-static-to-wrapper** skill to bulk-replace existing `File.*`/`Directory.*` call sites across your project.
